  "readmeFile": "ReadMe.md",
  "releaseNotes": "### 7.0.335\nFix: The managed AMS/TCP router no longer opens a second TCP connection to a remote system when local clients connect to several AmsPorts of the same target at the same time (e.g. 851 and 852). Concurrent connection establishments are now coalesced per target AmsNetId, and a connection can only unregister itself, so a duplicate connection can no longer tear down the connection that is actually in use. A remote connection that was closed by the remote system is now also evicted when a request finds it registered but no longer connected, so the request reconnects instead of failing with TargetMachineNotFound. Previously this could leave the router permanently unable to reach the target after a router restart, #330202\n### 7.0.314\nFix: The managed AMS/TCP router reuses its established remote connection for targets whose AmsNetId does not end in 1.1 (SubNet targets, e.g. a.b.c.d.2.1) again. Since 7.0.147 the connection registry used a different key for adding and for looking up a connection, so a new TCP connection was opened for every single request to such a target, which surfaced as ClientSyncTimeOut from the second request onwards, #323440\n### 7.0.286\nFix: The managed AMS/TCP router now rejects AMS/TCP frames whose declared body size (TcpHeader.cbSize) exceeds 64 MiB before allocating the receive buffer, and closes the connection. This prevents a memory-exhaustion denial-of-service where a crafted 6-byte header forced an arbitrarily large allocation.\n### 7.0.60\nEnh: AmsRouter not using app local UnixSocket path as fallback anymore.\nEnh: Using LoopbackConnection as fallback, when no UnixSocket path is specified by configuration.\n### 7.0.36\nBreaking Change: Removes .NET6 as Dependency (out-of-support)\n### 7.0\nEnh: Supports internal communication via UnixSockets or TCP/IP Loopback\nEnh: TargetFramework dependencies changed to net8.0, net6.0, netstandard2.0",
